00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.340
And we think Cloud Spanner
is a great database.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.100
So Cloud Spanner is the only
enterprise-grade, globally

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.190
distributed, and strongly
consistent database service

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.380
built for the cloud
specifically to combine

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.260
the benefits of relational
database structure

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.720
with non-relational
database scale.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.940
It's unique in the marketplace
in that it combines

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:56.390
transactions, SQL query,
and relational structure

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:57.980
with the scalability
you typically

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.440
associate with non-relational
or NoSQL databases.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.570
Databases are part
of every application.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.080
So it's very important to
pick the right database

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.340
for your application.

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:14.710
So there are a few things that
are tricky about databases

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.350
that Cloud Spanner
attempts to solve.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.140
The first has to do with scale.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.470
Most databases today are
only vertically scalable.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.770
You can add more
CPU memory or disk,

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.590
but that incurs
downtime typically.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.270
You can also shard
your database,

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.060
but that incurs
operational complexity

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:40.270
and has a manageability
aspect that's difficult.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.820
Next, operations are tricky.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.250
You can have hardware
that breaks down,

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.560
OS and software patches
that need to be applied.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.100
There are end-of-life
issues with software

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.770
that need to be managed
and accounted for.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.650
And all of those
also incur downtime.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.860
Last, you always constantly
need to tweak the performance

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.230
of the database to
get the most out of it

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:04.240
and get everything
that you want to be

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.802
able to accomplish with that.

00:03:05.802 --> 00:03:07.510
And last, there's the
infamous rebuilding

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:12.940
of indexes, which is a
dreaded task by a lot of DBAs.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.040
It also could incur downtime.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.680
Replication is another
area that's tricky.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:22.340
Making sure all copies of
the data are up to date

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:27.310
across different replicas,
providing read-only replicas

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.750
for reducing latency,
and increasing

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.700
the throughput of
your application,

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.650
as well as ensuring disaster
recovery using replication.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.440
All those are very tricky
things to get right.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.720
Keeping them in
sync is non-trivial,

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.640
and there's always a finite
replication delay when

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.390
you try to do this on your own.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:51.920
Last, transactions, particularly
around read/write transactions,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.150
are hard to do when you are
sharding and replicating

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.410
your database.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.680
Ensuring strong consistency
makes a developer's life

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.160
much simpler, because they
can rely on strong consistency

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.890
instead of having to
write in application logic

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:11.360
to deal with the constraints
around eventual consistency.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:17.800
So all four of these activities
make databases tricky today.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.410
Cloud Spanner is the first
and only relational database

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.450
that's globally
consistent, horizontally

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:24.560
scalable, and fully managed.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.200
So you don't have to compromise.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.360
You don't have to
make trade-offs

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:34.420
to get all of these aspects
around performance, scale, SQL,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:35.830
and manageability.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.680
With Cloud Spanner, you enjoy
all the traditional benefits

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.130
of a SQL database;
transactions, high availability,

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.520
schema changes without
downtime, and SQL queries.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.770
But unlike many
relational databases,

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.000
Cloud Spanner scales
horizontally from one

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.780
to thousands of servers, an
arbitrarily large number.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.970
With fully automatic data
replication and server

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.220
redundancy, Cloud
Spanner delivers up

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.580
to five nines of availability
in multi-region instances

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.760
and four nines of availability
in regional instances.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.040
In fact, Google's
internal Spanner instance

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.470
has been handling millions
of queries per second

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:15.020
from many Google
services for years.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.740
We've been running various
services on Spanner

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:19.710
for many, many years.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.010
So it's battle-tested,
and tried and true.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.730
Cloud Spanner combines
relational semantics

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:30.920
found in traditional
relational databases

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.510
with horizontal scale associated
with non-relational databases.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.220
Cloud Spanner is the
no-compromise database.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.650
You can have your
cake and eat it, too.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.300
Many other databases
out there today

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.510
approximate aspects of what
Cloud Spanner enables, but they

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.670
don't quite fully get there.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:50.930
And you see in the market
today lots of existing database

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:54.110
services adding on
additional features

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:56.900
to try to approximate what
Cloud Spanner achieves,

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.930
but Cloud Spanner solves these
fundamentally challenging

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:01.550
problems at the core.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.970
So these challenges
are not being addressed

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:08.150
as add-ons, but as something
that's fundamental to what

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:09.740
the database does.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.570
Cloud Spanner has
relational semantics

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.900
with schemas, enforced data
types, strong consistency,

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:19.200
and ACID transactions across
rows, tables, regions.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.230
SQL query using ANSI
2011 SQL with extensions.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.150
You also get all this
with horizontal scale

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:29.360
so you can scale to arbitrary
large database sizes.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.430
So combining
relational semantics

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:37.590
and horizontal scale all in
one easy-to-use database.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.390
So let's take a step
back a little bit.

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:41.270
Let's talk a little bit
about the philosophy

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:42.380
of Cloud Spanner.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.000
And it's super
important to understand

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.760
the philosophy of
Cloud Spanner in order

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:50.000
to understand the way it works.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.060
And that will help determine
if Cloud Spanner is

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.130
the right database
for your application.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:00.340
So first and foremost,
with Cloud Spanner,

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:04.180
the application is in
control of its database.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.940
The database is there
to store the data,

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.370
and to ensure that
data is strongly

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:13.180
consistent and replicated
and always available.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:15.460
In traditional
relational databases,

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.040
the database was in
control of the data,

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.250
enforcing value constraints,
doing data verification

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:28.150
with triggers and stored procs
and very fine-grained ACLs.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:33.820
With Cloud Spanner, all
of that application logic

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:35.700
is pushed into the application.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:38.230
You have to consider that
when moving from one database

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:39.880
to Cloud Spanner.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.590
So with Cloud Spanner,
the application

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:45.730
determines what's valid,
such as not null constraints

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:49.480
and unique indexes being
controlled with Spanner.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:52.030
And then there is no
real business logic

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:53.560
within the database layer.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:57.160
So the application has
to control that and use

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.470
Cloud Spanner for
what it is built for,

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.950
which is storing data.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.780
So let's dive into the
implications of this philosophy

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:07.910
and what it means
for an application

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:10.400
to be in control
and the database

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.960
to do things that
databases should be doing.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.640
So what workloads fit
best with Cloud Spanner?

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.630
And first and foremost, sharded
relational database systems,

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.830
such as sharded MySQL.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:29.350
Any time where you have a
sharded relational database

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:34.720
that is difficult to maintain,
that is becoming unwieldy,

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.650
that's a perfect fit for moving
to Cloud Spanner, because Cloud

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:42.130
Spanner gives you that scale
and that familiar relational

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:47.890
semantics and that SQL
queryability, but it removes

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.740
all of the management
overhead, all

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.670
of the operational headaches
associated with sharded MySQL

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:56.750
and manually sharding.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:00.170
And it's just sort of
a natural progression.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:05.930
And even in the original reasons
why Cloud Spanner was built

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:10.460
was because Google was using
a sharded MySQL database

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.550
and realized that it was just
too difficult to continue

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:14.570
to maintain that.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:18.080
So that was one of the
primary drivers for building

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.430
Spanner in the first place.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:23.720
The second reason and the second
workload that fits really well

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:26.030
with Cloud Spanner
is anytime you

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:29.780
have scalable relational
data and you're running out

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.610
of headroom in your
relational database

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:34.820
and you're considering moving
towards a NoSQL database,

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.530
or a non-relational store.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:40.850
And when you move to a
non-relational database,

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.770
you often have to give up.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.342
You often have to
make a compromise.

00:09:44.342 --> 00:09:45.800
You often have to
make a trade-off.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:50.180
And whether that's in terms
of consistency, manageability,

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:54.890
transactions, or any
number of things,

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.980
people are willing to
make that trade-off often

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.730
in order to get scale as
their application grows.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.740
But with Cloud
Spanner, you don't

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:05.060
have to make that trade-off.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.400
You can get the scale
you need, as well as

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:10.310
the familiar
relational semantics.

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:12.740
Last, manageability in HA.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.560
If you just want to get out
of the business of managing

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.800
your databases, Cloud
Spanner is a great fit.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.030
It's a very easy-to-use
system, particularly

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:25.430
for how complicated
it is under the hood.

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:28.200
We abstract away all of the
operations of the database.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.560
So there's no such thing
as planned downtime

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:31.730
with Cloud Spanner.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:34.655
Everything is an
online operation.

00:10:34.655 --> 00:10:38.660
All patching and
upgrades are online.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:41.060
Index rebuilds are online.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.760
Adding an index, schema
changes, all of that

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:45.500
is an online operation.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:48.410
And so you can get out of
the business of managing.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.960
The same is true for
providing high availability.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:53.330
Cloud Spanner provides
that out of the box.

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:56.180
We replicate your data
multiple ways depending

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:57.920
on the configuration
that you choose

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:00.890
and create when you set up
your Cloud Spanner instance.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:04.100
But we handle that
all for you so you

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.660
don't have to worry about it.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:10.550
And we provide SLAs,
high availability SLAs,

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:13.620
to guarantee that your
data will always be there.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:16.930
Next is multi-region.

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:20.290
With Cloud Spanner you can
write once and have the database

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.440
automatically replicate your
data to multiple regions.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:24.940
Whether that's for
high availability

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.400
or for reducing
read latency, that

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.860
depends on the needs
of your application.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:33.430
We have multiple multi-region
configurations for you

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:36.160
to choose from, depending on
the needs of your application.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:42.350
And we're always adding new
ones based on customer demand.

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:44.330
After saying that,
though, most customers

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.940
still use regional
instances today,

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:49.790
because you get the
high availability.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:52.280
We replicate your
data to multiple zones

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:55.010
within a given region.

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:58.490
And most customers are using
regional instances today,

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:01.520
but it's nice to know that
multi-region instances are

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.870
available should you need them.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:07.640
So let's take a look at where
Cloud Spanner is available.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:09.940
So as I mentioned,
most commonly used

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.670
are regional configurations.

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:16.240
We have Cloud Spanner
available in many, many GCP

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:18.220
regions across the globe.

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:20.660
And we're always
rolling out new regions.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.620
So for instance, just
last week with the launch

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:26.260
of the new Hong Kong
region, Cloud Spanner

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.180
was available in Hong Kong.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:31.390
And we'll be announcing
new regions again

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:35.890
in the near future where
Cloud Spanner is available.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:41.510
And so wherever
your application is,

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:45.290
there's a Cloud Spanner instance
available to you nearby.

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:48.800
Regional configurations are what
the majority of customers use.

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:50.480
They put their
application workload

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:52.790
in the same region as
the Spanner instance

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:54.470
to reduce latency.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:58.610
And then your data is
spread over several zones

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:02.780
within the region so that the
app has the same availability

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:03.620
as the database.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:05.110
And that's important
to remember.

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.820
The database is just
one particular part

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:12.020
of your application, one
place where availability is.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.960
But if your app goes down, and
the database is still there,

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.870
then users still can
use your application.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:22.040
So as I mentioned, regional
instances have four nines SLA.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:26.510
And coming soon, we'll
be adding new regions.

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:31.600
So we have a number of
multi-region configurations

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.710
depending on where your
application users are

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:37.420
and what your needs are.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:40.030
So this first one,
nam3, it's called,

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:43.600
is multi-region configuration
on the East Coast of the United

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:46.840
States in north Virginia
and South Carolina.

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:51.970
And multi-region instances
are a bit more expensive

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:55.240
than regional instances,
but you do get

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:56.940
an extra nine of availability.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.680
So with all these
multi-region configurations

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:03.400
that I'll go through, you get
five nines of availability.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:05.260
That's less than five
minutes of downtime

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:08.800
per year, which is
pretty extraordinary,

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:12.940
considering what it takes to get
that level of high availability

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:16.210
with an on-premises database
and to guarantee that level

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:18.110
of high availability.

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.770
So this is the first
multi-region configuration.

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:24.250
We also have another North
America configuration

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:27.310
that has two
read-write regions, one

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.530
in South Carolina, one in Iowa.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:32.320
And then it also has
two read-only regions;

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:36.950
one in Oregon, and one
in our new LA region.

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:40.960
And so this configuration
is great for data

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.960
that you need
replicated across the US

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:49.570
for an app that is used
predominantly in North America.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:52.420
But because of the
Google network,

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:55.810
it could also serve data
at pretty fast speeds

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:58.100
across any ocean.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:00.610
And as your data is replicated
on both sides of the country,

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:05.830
or both sides of the US,
it has closer proximity

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:11.140
to cables crossing the Pacific
as well as the Atlantic.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:13.300
There's also a
multi-region configuration

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:17.080
in Europe with read-write
replicas in the Netherlands

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:20.950
and Belgium for
European-centric workloads.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:23.320
And this provides five
nines of availability

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:27.220
while still keeping
your data in Europe.

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:33.920
So that's a great option for
customers based out of Europe.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:36.660
And last is a global
configuration.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:41.810
So this configuration has
the read-write regions

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:45.230
within the United States,
in Iowa and Oklahoma,

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:48.600
and then read-only regions
in Europe and Asia.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:50.780
So for a truly
global applications,

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:55.220
this configuration
is perfect for you.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.650
Now, it's really
interesting to think about

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:00.500
whether you need a
multi-region configuration,

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.050
or you just need multiple
regional configurations.

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:06.650
And that really depends on
the needs of your application

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:08.700
and what you're
trying to accomplish,

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:12.320
whether you're trying to
have your data replicated

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:16.310
around the world so you can
have very low latency data

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:22.670
access across the world, or
if you can sort of separate

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:25.940
your users out and
have each country

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:28.940
sort of operate separately.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:32.060
And then you can use multiple
regional instances as opposed

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:35.300
to a global configuration or
multi-region configuration.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:38.330
So the choice is up to
you, but Cloud Spanner

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:39.230
is here to support.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:46.300
So let's talk a
little bit about when

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:49.630
Cloud Spanner fits less well.

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:55.090
And the next few slides
might seem negative.

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:58.840
I'm a marketer, but this is
not supposed to be negative.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:01.630
I really want to be
honest with you all today

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:07.930
and provide insight into where
Cloud Spanner excels today

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:10.510
and where it may
require a little bit

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:14.020
more work to be able to use.

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:17.740
Because Cloud Spanner provides
relational semantics and scale,

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.800
it seems like the
perfect solution.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.000
And it is for many workloads.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.150
But there are a
few instances where

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.579
it may require a bit more
work and a bit more thought

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:28.790
on your part.

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:31.090
So the first is lift and shift.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:36.650
And this really comes
down to the philosophy

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:38.180
of Cloud Spanner.

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:40.520
It's not a compatible database.

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:44.060
You can't just take a MySQL
application or database

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:46.340
and lift it up and put
it on Cloud Spanner.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:48.650
It doesn't have compatibility.

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:52.910
It doesn't do some of the
things that MySQL does.

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:55.700
And for some
traditional databases

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:59.060
that may have 30
years of features

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:02.060
that have been added
over time, Cloud Spanner

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:05.450
doesn't have a lot
of those features,

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:08.600
because of the
philosophical differences

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:11.270
between Cloud Spanner and
traditional relational

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:11.970
databases.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:18.730
So customers who have
a lot of business logic

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:24.130
in their database using
triggers, stored procedures,

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:28.000
have a hard time moving to
Cloud Spanner sometimes,

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:30.760
because they have to
take that business logic

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:33.380
and move it back
into the application.

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:36.340
The application is in
charge of business logic.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:40.480
The application is
in charge of knowing

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:42.460
when something should happen.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:44.800
The database is
there to store data,

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.990
and make sure it's
available, and make sure

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:49.330
that it's replicated.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:55.540
So Cloud Spanner does not have
triggers and stored procedures.

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:58.170
This is not necessarily
a blocker, though.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:01.140
Some customers see the benefit
of rewriting their code

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:03.120
to optimize it for the cloud.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:05.670
And so it's sometimes
a great opportunity

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:09.390
to re-examine some
of the legacy code

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.190
that may exist in
your code base,

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:17.090
and then sort of modernize
it for a cloud design point.

00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:21.110
The next is compatibility.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:26.900
Customers who want
rapid lift and shift,

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:29.330
migrating to Cloud
Spanner may not

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:33.080
be a trivial task when you
just want to flip something up

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:34.100
to the cloud.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.440
And so what we see
a lot of the times

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:42.680
is folks moving to something
like Cloud SQL PostgreS first,

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:46.490
and then modernizing over
time to get to Cloud Spanner.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:49.670
This is certainly easier
today with the enhancements

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:55.910
that have been made around DML
and an upgraded JDBC driver.

00:19:55.910 --> 00:20:04.740
But all of the tools and all
of the other ecosystem things

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:08.430
that you might rely on, you
need to take a look at that

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:11.820
and see what you rely on and
what your constraints are

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:13.350
when moving to Cloud Spanner.

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:18.210
And we're always adding new
drivers and new ORM tools,

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:21.780
and the rest of the ecosystem.

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:25.260
But just take a look and
see what's available today,

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:27.150
see what's on the
roadmap for tomorrow,

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:32.910
and see if there's a path that's
compatible with what you need.

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:34.830
And the last thing
I want to mention

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:39.210
is apps that need
super low latency,

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:43.140
Cloud Spanner isn't the
right choice for you.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.270
That's not to say that
Cloud Spanner isn't fast.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:46.780
It's very fast.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:51.240
But when you're talking about
microsecond, nanosecond, very

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:53.910
low, single-digit
millisecond latency,

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:58.320
Cloud Spanner probably isn't
the right tool for that job.

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:03.000
Other services, like
Cloud Bigtable, or even

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.569
in-memory services, like
Cloud Memorystore for Redis

00:21:06.569 --> 00:21:08.610
are probably the thing
you want to look at if you

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:12.180
need ultra low latency.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:15.150
Cloud Spanner
provides low latency,

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:19.470
but not at that very low level.

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:23.820
You can also get very
sustained and consistent levels

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:26.550
of latency with Cloud
Spanner as you scale up,

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:28.260
which is one of
the nice features

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:30.270
that customers love about it.

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:32.990
As they add more nodes
to their instance

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:39.540
and their configuration, they
see the latency keep up and not

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:44.040
escalate as the load on
their database grows.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:45.990
So those are some of
the key areas where

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:48.630
Cloud Spanner fits less well.

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:51.420
Let's talk about some of
the common misconceptions

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:52.470
around Cloud Spanner.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:55.650
And this is really where we
get into the questions that

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:58.200
get asked around
Cloud Spanner a lot.

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:01.710
And it's funny,
because when people

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:04.350
start looking at Cloud
Spanner, most people

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:06.990
have the same common
set of questions.

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:09.610
And I want to
address those today.

00:22:09.610 --> 00:22:12.400
The first is that, yes,
this is fully managed.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:17.070
There is no software patching,
no upgrades, no downtime,

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:19.620
no index rebuilds,
no resharding,

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:22.170
and you can dynamically
scale up and down.

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:24.750
In fact, it only
takes four clicks

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:26.940
to create a Cloud
Spanner instance.

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:29.520
You just go into
the GCP console.

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:31.410
And you create your
Spanner instance.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:32.860
You give it a name.

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:35.040
You pick how many
nodes you want.

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:37.290
You pick what configuration
type you want,

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:41.430
whether that's regional
and in what region,

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:44.090
or if you want a
multi-region instance.

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:45.500
And then you click Create.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:51.330
And in a matter of seconds you
have a fully managed, fully

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:56.150
replicated, scalable database.

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:59.340
And what's really nice about
it is as your application needs

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:01.800
grow, you can just go
back into the console,

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:04.800
or you can also do it
programmatically as well,

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:09.160
and increase or decrease
the size of your instance.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:15.060
You can just go in and click one
to however many times you need,

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:17.880
and get more throughput
for your instance.

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:22.050
And in a matter of seconds,
you have additional throughput.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:25.140
It sometimes takes a couple
minutes for Cloud Spanner

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:29.730
to repartition and
balance out your data,

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:33.000
but this is something that
the database is always

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.820
doing on the back end anyways.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:41.170
Repartitioning, resharding,
electing new leaders

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:43.900
within the system
on the back end

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:46.910
is something that is
constantly happening.

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:49.270
It's constantly going
on within the system.

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:50.560
It's not a failure event.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:52.070
It's not a scale event.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:54.790
This is just something that
the database is always doing.

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:57.020
So as you add more
nodes, it just says,

00:23:57.020 --> 00:24:02.190
hey, I've got more nodes to add
to my Cloud Spanner instance,

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:04.300
and it balances the data out.

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:06.370
And that takes a
couple of minutes.

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:10.630
But compare that to what
it's like increasing capacity

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:12.130
on an on-premise
database where you

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:15.850
have to order capacity,
wait for it to arrive,

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:18.850
take it out of the box,
plug it in, set it up.

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:20.500
I mean, we're talking months.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:23.610
Cloud Spanner gives
you this in minutes.

00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:30.040
It's really a different type
of management experience.

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:38.980
And for operators who
want to be able to control

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:41.950
every single aspect
of the database

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:46.270
and know where every block
of data is stored on disk,

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:48.900
Cloud Spanner is fully managed.

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:51.940
So you're not going to get
that level of granularity

00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:53.260
of control.

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:56.020
So it does require
a bit of a shift

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:59.710
in thinking around
what it is that you

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:03.520
spend your time on when it
comes to managing the database.

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:07.150
But as I'll get to in
the next couple slides,

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:09.430
there's still a lot to think
about and a lot to plan

00:25:09.430 --> 00:25:10.810
for in using Cloud Spanner.

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:18.200
The next common misconception
is around strong consistency.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:22.110
And this is really a
philosophical aspect

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:24.640
of Cloud Spanner as well.

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:29.760
This comes to improving
the developer experience.

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:33.300
Being able to rely
on strong consistency

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:37.410
is super important and super
powerful to a developer.

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:40.380
With a lot of sharded
databases, with a lot

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:43.380
of scale-out
databases, particularly

00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:49.860
those that cover a
wide geographic area,

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:54.240
those databases use something
called eventual consistency.

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:57.310
Or maybe there are multiple
flavors of consistency,

00:25:57.310 --> 00:26:01.680
but there aren't any that
offer strong consistency

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.170
across regions
like Cloud Spanner.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:08.620
And when you have to reason
over eventual consistency,

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:11.200
you have to build in
complicated checks

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:13.880
into your application
code to say, hey,

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:14.800
is this a strong read?

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:16.600
Hey, is this an eventual read?

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:17.920
Is this the latest update?

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:20.500
Can I trust what this thing is?

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:24.220
With Cloud Spanner, everything
is a strongly consistent read.

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:28.300
You can do bounded stale reads
if you need to improve latency,

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:31.280
but your data is still
strongly consistent.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:35.170
You're never going to get
anything that's out of order.

00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:36.790
If your application
is OK with it,

00:26:36.790 --> 00:26:39.520
you might just get something
that's a few seconds older,

00:26:39.520 --> 00:26:41.250
but not out of order.

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:46.030
And so this is really
an enhanced version

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:49.330
of strong consistency
called external consistency,

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:51.100
where we talk about
external as being

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:53.830
able to look at an
external wall clock

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:57.520
and see what time something
happened within the database.

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:00.760
And that's really the foundation
of how Cloud Spanner works.

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:04.540
It relies on a combination
of software and hardware

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:08.610
to enable this low latency
synchronous replication.

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.530
So data is always
available on commit.

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:15.790
And writes happen
at super low latency

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:19.990
once a quorum of all
regions within an instance

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:22.180
have accepted the write.

00:27:22.180 --> 00:27:24.370
And so this provides
a true ACID database

00:27:24.370 --> 00:27:29.230
that spans multiple regions or
multiple zones within a region,

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:31.930
offering strong consistency.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:35.980
And this is possible through the
high speed network that Google

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:38.710
has built out, as
well as relying

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:44.200
on a fully optimized stack,
including TrueTime, which you

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:47.830
may have heard about, which
uses atomic clocks and GPS

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:55.300
satellites to provide very,
very accurate and pinpoint time.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:28:01.540
So different replicas that
span a wide geographic area

00:28:01.540 --> 00:28:05.710
can still take the same time
and reduce the uncertainty

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:09.730
around when something happens.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:11.940
So let's talk about a
couple common issues

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:16.500
that customers face when
moving to Cloud Spanner.

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:19.440
And the first is really
around key choice.

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:22.620
Your key choice is
super important.

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:28.440
Sequential timestamp-based keys
can cause hotspotting, which

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:29.920
can impede your performance.

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:32.310
So you want to be very
careful around that.

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:36.540
Random UUIDs or hashes
distribute the data better.

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:41.200
Some sort of composite around
random UUIDs work well, too.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:45.720
So it's very important to think
through what schema you're

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:49.560
going to choose and your
schema design when moving

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:50.490
to Cloud Spanner.

00:28:50.490 --> 00:28:52.350
And that's a bit of
a shift in thinking

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:55.800
from traditional
relational databases.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.800
Next, as I mentioned
before, lack of triggers,

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:01.740
stored procedures,
and check constraints

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:05.670
mean that you have to move that
logic into the application.

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:09.180
So Not Null constraints
exist, and keys

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:12.900
are unique, and unique
column values via indexes.

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.640
But you need to
think through how

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:17.050
you get all of that
application logic out

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:19.080
and put that in your
application and use

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.390
the database for storing data.

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:24.900
A couple of common issues
also to just mention,

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:27.450
foreign key constraints
and on-delete cascades

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.480
only exist with parent-child
interleaved tables.

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:33.810
So Cloud Spanner has
an interesting concept

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:38.700
that's similar to a pre-join
called interleave tables, which

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:41.100
we don't have time to
get into in detail today,

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:44.400
but it allows you to
signal to the system

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:47.700
that you want data
physically stored next

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:53.010
to each other, physically
stored on the same split

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:54.330
within the database.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:59.250
And that allows for data that's
commonly accessed together

00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:02.310
to be accessed faster
than it would if it was

00:30:02.310 --> 00:30:03.784
stored on a different split.

00:30:03.784 --> 00:30:05.700
And that's just an
optimization, but something

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:07.991
to think through as you're
designing your Cloud Spanner

00:30:07.991 --> 00:30:10.260
schema.

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:11.610
More common issues.

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:14.340
Indexes are tables too.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:17.790
So you have the same
constraints for index keys

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:21.780
as you do for your
primary key, where

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:25.710
you don't want to just
use an incrementing value,

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:28.710
because that will
lead to hotspotting.

00:30:28.710 --> 00:30:31.590
Next, your primary
key is the row ID.

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:33.750
So it uniquely
identifies the row,

00:30:33.750 --> 00:30:36.760
and it's used to determine
where the row is in storage.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:38.250
And that's really
why hotspotting

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:40.980
happens with Cloud
Spanner, because we

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:43.830
group rows that are next
to each other together

00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:48.150
and store them on the same split
to allow for faster access.

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:49.955
And if you're always writing--

00:30:49.955 --> 00:30:51.330
sequentially,
you're always going

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:53.490
to be writing to the
same node, as opposed

00:30:53.490 --> 00:30:57.030
to allowing the system to
distribute that load evenly

00:30:57.030 --> 00:30:59.460
across multiple nodes.

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:03.120
Last, backup and
disaster recovery.

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:05.850
We're always improving
in this area.

00:31:05.850 --> 00:31:08.490
And the system obviously
takes system backups

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:10.320
and we handle high
availability and we

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:12.580
handle replication for you.

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:15.720
But in terms of
managed backup tools

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:21.220
that you might be familiar
with in your existing database,

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:23.220
we're working on that still.

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:26.400
Today, there's managed
export and import.

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:28.770
We take one hour of
historical data snapshots,

00:31:28.770 --> 00:31:30.390
which it could go back to.

00:31:30.390 --> 00:31:33.390
And we have a system-managed
backup service

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:34.860
coming very soon.

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:37.620
But it's a little bit
different than what

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:41.260
you might be expecting
compared to on-premise today.

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:44.370
So just something to think
about, but obviously not

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:45.645
an impediment to usage.

00:31:48.550 --> 00:31:50.370
So some common
questions that we always

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:52.350
get around Cloud Spanner.

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:54.720
What's the replication delay?

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:57.600
You're moving data around the
world, around the country,

00:31:57.600 --> 00:31:58.740
around the region.

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.680
This thing obviously
has to be slow.

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:04.240
And effectively,
there is no delay.

00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:06.510
As soon as a
transaction commits,

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:10.030
reads from the replicas
will have the latest data.

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:13.560
And we guarantee that because
before you get the act back

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:16.110
or about your write,
that data has committed

00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:19.770
to a quorum of the replicas.

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:23.340
So every replica knows
what is the latest

00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:24.990
and if it has the latest data.

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:29.670
So you're guaranteed not
to get old or stale data

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:30.900
unless you want it.

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:33.840
As I mentioned, you can
get bounded stale reads,

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:37.800
in which case you're OK with
slightly out-of-date data.

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:40.110
In practicality, it's
a few milliseconds

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:43.260
with the replication delays, and
it's just seen as query latency

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:45.580
from the application.

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:49.800
The other question
that we often get

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:52.740
is when to use Cloud
Spanner versus Cloud

00:32:52.740 --> 00:32:54.960
SQL versus Cloud Bigtable.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:56.610
And this really comes
down to, what do

00:32:56.610 --> 00:32:58.950
you want out of your database?

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:01.890
If you want a managed,
transactional, queryable,

00:33:01.890 --> 00:33:07.470
infinitely scalable, no downtime
database, use Cloud Spanner.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:09.750
If you need something
that's transactional,

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:13.920
queryable, you can tolerate
occasional maintenance

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:17.790
and downtime, but you're
willing to sacrifice

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:21.390
that for compatibility
with MySQL and PostgreS,

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:24.690
and you're OK with limited
scalability, limited headroom,

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:27.190
Cloud SQL is perfect for that.

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:29.220
And if you need
super low latency

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:32.370
and you're willing to
trade off queryability,

00:33:32.370 --> 00:33:37.830
Cloud Bigtable is the database
or the data store for you.

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:40.290
And choosing between
the three of those

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:43.410
really comes down to
what are the unique needs

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:45.510
of your application
and what you need

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:49.530
to be able to accomplish
with your database.

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.099
Some additional questions.

00:33:51.099 --> 00:33:52.140
This one always comes up.

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:54.540
Why is Cloud Spanner
so expensive?

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:59.090
And really, it's sort of
that initial sticker shock.

00:33:59.090 --> 00:34:00.750
But once you start
to think through it

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:04.110
and compare Cloud Spanner
to other databases,

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:06.360
it's really not so
expensive, particularly

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:08.889
compared to on-premises.

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:11.010
And you have to think
about what you're getting

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.350
when you buy Cloud Spanner.

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:15.719
For the minimum
production configuration,

00:34:15.719 --> 00:34:18.300
you're getting 6
terabytes of storage.

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:21.830
You're getting up to 30,000
QPS of reads, up to 6,000

00:34:21.830 --> 00:34:23.100
QPS of writes.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:27.870
Each node is
replicated three times.

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:30.989
So in a three-node
cluster, your data

00:34:30.989 --> 00:34:35.370
is spread across nine nodes
and in three different zones.

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:37.830
And it's fully managed,
fully backed up, fully

00:34:37.830 --> 00:34:39.120
replicated for you.

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:43.440
So you're getting a lot that's
all baked into that price.

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:47.760
It's just not broken out in
terms of buying a core thing

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:50.130
and then adding on
additional functionality.

00:34:50.130 --> 00:34:52.259
It's all just one price.

00:34:52.259 --> 00:34:56.340
The next question is, is
Cloud Spanner SQL compliant?

00:34:56.340 --> 00:34:59.370
Yes, it's ANSI 2011
with extensions.

00:34:59.370 --> 00:35:04.900
We support insert, update,
delete, select SQL statements.

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:08.340
It's very familiar
to folks moving

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:12.540
from traditional relational
databases to Cloud Spanner.

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:14.370
And then after saying
all of these things,

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:17.730
people often ask, is Cloud
Spanner really relational?

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:18.570
Yes, it is.

00:35:18.570 --> 00:35:21.900
It provides strong consistency,
strongly typed schemas,

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:24.450
SQL queries with
INNER/OUTER JOIN.

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:29.320
And so it really is a
scalable relational database.

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:32.220
There are some philosophical
differences at its core

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:36.480
that change the
way that you have

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:38.560
to think through
using the database,

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:41.170
but it's still a
relational database.

00:35:41.170 --> 00:35:42.180
It's still scalable.

00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:45.000
It's still SQL-- all the
stuff that's familiar

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:47.300
and you're used to.

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:51.890
And it's part of a broader
portfolio of fully managed data

00:35:51.890 --> 00:35:52.800
services.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:56.930
So from all the way having
a managed in-memory data

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:59.690
service for Redis in Cloud
Memorystore to having

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.630
non-relational databases
in Cloud Datastore,

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:05.870
Cloud Firestore for mobile
applications, mobile web

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:08.660
and IT application
development, to Cloud Bigtable,

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:11.510
which is a wide column database
service for super low latency

00:36:11.510 --> 00:36:13.382
data access.

00:36:13.382 --> 00:36:14.840
And then on the
relational side, we

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:18.230
have Cloud SQL, which is
managed MySQL and PostgreS,

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:20.390
and Cloud Spanner,
which is what we've

00:36:20.390 --> 00:36:21.770
been talking about
today in terms

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:23.690
of a scalable
relational database.

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:26.330
And then for object store,
we have Cloud Storage.

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:28.310
And for data
warehousing, we have

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:31.010
BigQuery, which is our
enterprise data warehouse

00:36:31.010 --> 00:36:32.240
offering.

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:34.670
So I hope you enjoyed this talk.

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:37.670
I hope it was helpful in
terms of thinking through how

00:36:37.670 --> 00:36:43.100
Cloud Spanner might be the
right tool for your application,

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:44.820
the right database
for your application.

00:36:44.820 --> 00:36:49.700
I hope that you thought
this was helpful to think

00:36:49.700 --> 00:36:51.980
through some of the
questions you might have.

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:54.830
And now please stay
tuned for a live Q&amp;A.

00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:56.330
We'll be back in
less than a minute.

00:36:56.330 --> 00:36:56.830
Thank you.

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:07.705
Welcome back.

00:38:07.705 --> 00:38:09.580
So we've got some
questions from the audience

00:38:09.580 --> 00:38:12.050
that we're going to answer here.

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:14.020
So the first one is,
what's so special

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:16.000
about strong consistency?

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:20.550
And really, this
comes down to making

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:22.950
developers' lives easier.

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:26.650
If they can assume
strong consistency,

00:38:26.650 --> 00:38:31.600
they can assume that
every read after a write

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:34.240
is the latest and greatest data.

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:38.930
And that just
makes life simpler.

00:38:38.930 --> 00:38:40.600
There's a lot that's
been published

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:47.680
on even how eventual consistency
can lead to bugs and security

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:53.320
holes, but that's sort
of on the extreme end.

00:38:53.320 --> 00:38:56.560
But at its core,
strong consistency

00:38:56.560 --> 00:39:03.550
is just a nice aspect
to have for developers.

00:39:03.550 --> 00:39:07.240
Developers that rely on
eventual consistency,

00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:09.070
if they're building
multiple applications,

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:11.500
they have to solve
the problem of dealing

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:14.650
with eventual consistency
for every application,

00:39:14.650 --> 00:39:18.290
whereas Cloud Spanner solves
that problem in the database.

00:39:18.290 --> 00:39:21.190
So Cloud Spanner does
what databases do really

00:39:21.190 --> 00:39:24.280
well in terms of storing
data and providing

00:39:24.280 --> 00:39:29.290
the latest and greatest version
back to the application.

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:31.560
So the next question is,
why doesn't Cloud Spanner

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:33.390
have all the features
I'm used to having

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:35.280
in my on-prem database?

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:37.770
And so a lot of
on-prem databases

00:39:37.770 --> 00:39:40.300
have been around
for 30 or 40 years.

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.990
So there's been
plenty of time, lots

00:39:42.990 --> 00:39:47.250
of customer feedback in terms
of what features they want.

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:48.925
Also, philosophically,
these databases

00:39:48.925 --> 00:39:50.050
are a little bit different.

00:39:50.050 --> 00:39:54.870
These databases support
business logic in the database.

00:39:54.870 --> 00:39:57.690
The database is in control.

00:39:57.690 --> 00:40:00.420
With Cloud Spanner, the
application is in control.

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:03.720
And Cloud Spanner is just
there to store the data

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:06.360
and read/write the data.

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:11.860
Based on customer feedback
and customer input,

00:40:11.860 --> 00:40:15.000
we are always adding new
features to Cloud Spanner.

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:18.411
So if you have a feature that
you really need, please let us

00:40:18.411 --> 00:40:18.910
know.

00:40:18.910 --> 00:40:22.492
We want to know
what you guys need,

00:40:22.492 --> 00:40:24.450
particularly as you
modernize your applications

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:29.370
and move them to the
cloud, and use all of what

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:32.520
the cloud has to offer today.

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:35.100
The next question is, how
can I try out Cloud Spanner?

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:40.140
The easy answer there is to spin
up a Google Cloud free trial

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:42.930
and take a look
at Cloud Spanner.

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:47.550
While you can go look
at the pricing sheet

00:40:47.550 --> 00:40:49.710
and you said, oh,
well, I'm not going

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:52.290
to be able to use
anything in production,

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:54.540
the free trial is more
than enough to go in

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:57.150
and play with Cloud Spanner
and start playing around.

00:40:57.150 --> 00:40:58.700
Cloud Spanner is billed hourly.

00:40:58.700 --> 00:41:01.197
So just because you go in
and create an instance,

00:41:01.197 --> 00:41:03.030
doesn't mean that you're
going to get billed

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:05.320
for a month's worth of usage.

00:41:05.320 --> 00:41:08.770
The next question is, when do
you use Bigtable versus Cloud

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:09.660
Spanner?

00:41:09.660 --> 00:41:12.180
I touched on this a
little bit, but really,

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:16.690
it boils down to Bigtable is
for super high throughput,

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:19.860
low latency reads and writes.

00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:25.650
And that's great for IoT data,
for streaming data, time series

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:33.210
data, for using as the back
end store for graph stores.

00:41:33.210 --> 00:41:37.440
Bigtable makes a
lot of sense there.

00:41:37.440 --> 00:41:39.960
It's scalable in the
same way Spanner is,

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:45.660
but when it replicates,
it's eventually consistent.

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:50.090
The replication across zones
is eventually consistent.

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:54.650
As well as it doesn't have
the sophisticated queryability

00:41:54.650 --> 00:41:56.060
that Cloud Spanner does.

00:41:56.060 --> 00:42:02.150
So everything in Bigtable is a
point lookup or a table scan,

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:04.310
whereas Cloud Spanner
gives you the SQL

00:42:04.310 --> 00:42:08.060
queryability you'd expect
out of a relational database.

00:42:08.060 --> 00:42:11.090
That being said, though, there
are a number of use cases

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:14.280
where Bigtable is
the right choice.

00:42:14.280 --> 00:42:16.160
And it mostly has
to do with latency

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:19.160
and super high
throughput, where you just

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:21.870
need that blazing fast speed.

00:42:21.870 --> 00:42:24.420
And so the last
question here is,

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:27.420
I have a custom application
that runs on legacy databases.

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:28.530
Can I migrate to Spanner?

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:30.600
Is Spanner full-fledged RDBMS?

00:42:30.600 --> 00:42:32.640
And the short answer
is, yes, it is.

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:33.750
You can migrate.

00:42:33.750 --> 00:42:37.380
It's not going to be just
a straight lift and shift.

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:38.910
In moving to Cloud
Spanner, you're

00:42:38.910 --> 00:42:41.130
going to have to think
through, what are

00:42:41.130 --> 00:42:43.110
the aspects in your database?

00:42:43.110 --> 00:42:45.270
What's the business logic
that's in your database?

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:47.319
What are the queries you
have in your database?

00:42:47.319 --> 00:42:49.110
You'll have to modernize
that a little bit.

00:42:49.110 --> 00:42:51.330
But if you do have just
a simple application

00:42:51.330 --> 00:42:53.830
and it has a simple
usage of a database,

00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:55.710
there's no reason why
that wouldn't just

00:42:55.710 --> 00:42:57.470
move to Cloud Spanner.

00:42:57.470 --> 00:42:59.880
And then you can
optimize over time.

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:04.410
But we do see a lot of customers
that either think through

00:43:04.410 --> 00:43:07.350
and take the time to modernize
as they move to Cloud Spanner,

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:09.060
or they lift and
shift to Cloud SQL

00:43:09.060 --> 00:43:12.060
as a first step to get the
data into Google Cloud,

00:43:12.060 --> 00:43:14.400
and then take the time
and take the opportunity

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:19.470
to move to Cloud Spanner
as their needs grow.

00:43:19.470 --> 00:43:21.420
So those are all the
questions I have.

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:23.970
Please stay tuned for
the next session, which

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:27.870
is on how to enhance security
and privacy of your container

00:43:27.870 --> 00:43:28.800
infrastructure.

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:31.870
Thank you so much
for joining today.

00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:34.620
